<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · 1751 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">O Come, All Ye Faithful</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="la">Adeste Fideles</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Christmas carol first printed in Latin in 1751. Its English words, from 1841, are sung in churches around the world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>John Francis Wade (attributed); additional Latin verses by Jean-François-Étienne Borderies; English translation by Frederick Oakeley and William Thomas Brooke</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known; attributed to John Francis Wade, John Reading, Thomas Arne and others</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>English Catholics; later Christians of many churches</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>France, where John Francis Wade lived in exile</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first printed 1751</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (Latin words first printed by John Francis Wade in 1751 (he died in 1786); English translation by Frederick Oakeley, 1841)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-come-all-ye-faithful">O Come, All Ye Faithful</a>&quot;, first known by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> title &quot;Adeste Fideles&quot;, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> of uncertain origin. Its words have been credited at various times to St. Bonaventure in the 13th century, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> IV of Portugal in the 17th, and most often to Cistercian monks of the German, Portuguese or Spanish provinces of the order. A parody of its chorus was already current before 1542, when it appears in chapter 41 of Rabelais's Gargantua.</p><p>Modern English hymnals usually credit the text to John Francis Wade (1711–1786), whose name appears on the earliest printed versions. Wade, an English Catholic layman, lived in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where he had fled after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1745">Jacobite rising of 1745</a> was crushed, and made his living copying musical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> he found in libraries. In 1751 he published a printed collection of his copies, Cantus Diversi pro Dominicis et Festis per annum, the first printed source of the hymn: four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> verses, set in the square notation of medieval liturgical music and in triple time, unlike modern versions. A manuscript by Wade dating to 1751 is held by Stonyhurst College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>. Whether Wade wrote the hymn, copied it or reworked an older one is not known. The tune has likewise been credited to Wade, to John Reading and his son, to Handel, Gluck, Marcos Portugal, John IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-arne">Thomas Arne</a>, whom Wade knew.</p><p>Wade's liturgical books were often decorated with Jacobite imagery, and some English Catholic books of the time placed the hymn close to prayers for the exiled Stuart claimant. This has led to speculation, put forward most recently by Bennett Zon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-university">Durham University</a>, that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hymn was a coded birth ode and rallying cry for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a>. The theory rests on claims that are unproven, and the hymn draws almost all of its content from the Bible and the Nicene Creed; the Irish musicologist William H. Grattan Flood concluded that its words and music came from a Catholic source for Catholic worship.</p><p>The hymn was printed again in the 1760 edition of Evening Offices of the Church and in Samuel Webbe's An Essay on the Church Plain Chant (1782). Later in the 18th century the French priest Jean-François-Étienne Borderies added three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> verses, and another anonymous Latin verse is rarely printed. In 1795 the Duke of Leeds heard it sung at the chapel of the Portuguese embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, now Our Lady of the Assumption and St Gregory, Warwick Street, and it was sometimes called the &quot;Portuguese Hymn&quot;. Friedrich Heinrich Ranke published a German translation in 1823.</p><p>The English Catholic priest Frederick Oakeley translated it into English in 1841; at his Margaret Chapel in Marylebone it was first sung under another title, before it took its present form. The version most sung today joins one of Oakeley's translations of the four original verses with William Thomas Brooke's translation of three of the added ones, and was first published in Murray's Hymnal in 1852. It became widespread in English-speaking countries, and it has been translated into many other languages. &quot;Adeste Fideles&quot; is traditionally the final anthem of Midnight Mass at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica</a> in the Vatican.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> hymn that calls the faithful to come joyfully to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a> and worship the newborn Christ. Its verses restate the Church's teaching on the person of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, drawn from the Bible and the Nicene Creed, and each ends in a refrain of adoration. The mood is festive and triumphant.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Adeste fideles læti triumphantes,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, faithful ones, joyful and triumphant,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Venite, venite in Bethlehem.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>come, come into Bethlehem.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Natum videte</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>See him born,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Regem angelorum:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the King of angels:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Venite adoremus (3×)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, let us adore (3×)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dominum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Lord.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Deum de Deo, lumen de lumine</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God from God, light from light,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Gestant puellæ viscera</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the maiden's womb carries</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Deum verum, genitum non factum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>true God, begotten, not made.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Venite adoremus (3×)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, let us adore (3×)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dominum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Lord.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Cantet nunc io, chorus angelorum;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let the choir of angels now sing out for joy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Cantet nunc aula cælestium,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let the court of the heavenly ones now sing:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Gloria, gloria in excelsis Deo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Glory, glory to God in the highest.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Venite adoremus (3×)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, let us adore (3×)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dominum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Lord.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 41 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ergo qui natus die hodierna.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So, you who are born on this day,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Jesu, tibi sit gloria,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Jesus, to you be glory,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Patris æterni Verbum caro factum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Word of the eternal Father, made flesh.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Venite adoremus (3×)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, let us adore (3×)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dominum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Lord.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">En grege relicto, humiles ad cunas,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lo, leaving the flock, the humble ones to the cradle,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Vocati pastores adproperant:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the shepherds who were called, make haste:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Et nos ovanti gradu festinemus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and let us too hurry with joyful step.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Venite adoremus (3×)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, let us adore (3×)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dominum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Lord.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Stella duce, Magi Christum adorantes,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With the star as guide, the Magi, adoring Christ,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Aurum, tus et myrrham dant munera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>give gold, incense and myrrh as gifts.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Iesu infanti corda præbeamus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us offer our hearts to the infant Jesus.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Venite adoremus (3×)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, let us adore (3×)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dominum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Lord.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Pro nobis egenum et fœno cubantem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Him, poor for us and lying in hay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Piis foveamus amplexibus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let us cherish with devoted embraces.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Sic nos amantem quis non redamaret?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who would not love in return one who loves us so?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Venite adoremus (3×)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, let us adore (3×)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dominum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Lord.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Æterni Parentis splendorem æternum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The eternal splendour of the eternal Parent</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Velatum sub carne videbimus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we shall see veiled under flesh,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Deum infantem pannis involutum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God an infant, wrapped in cloths.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Venite adoremus (3×)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, let us adore (3×)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dominum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Lord.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: John Francis Wade; the earliest printed Latin text (1751) and its later verses, as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from Wikipedia (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/O_Come%2C_All_Ye_Faithful" target="_blank" rel="noopener">O Come, All Ye Faithful</a>); public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>Refrain: 'Venite adoremus' is 'come, let us adore'; '(3×)' kept as in the original.</li><li>2.1 and 2.3 echo the Nicene Creed ('Deum de Deo, lumen de lumine ... genitum non factum').</li><li>3.1: 'io' is a Latin cry of joy, left untranslated.</li><li>4.1: the line as printed ends with a full stop; 'qui natus' is read as an address to Jesus, named in the next line.</li><li>5.1: 'En grege relicto' is literally 'lo, the flock left behind'; 'humiles ad cunas' may go with the shepherds of line 2 ('humble, to the cradle').</li><li>line 3.1: 'io' is a Latin cry of joy.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
